WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:07.299
On August 19, 2020, Russian politician, Alexei
Navalny was shooting this campaign video.

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:09.309
He was in Siberia.

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:14.059
One of the many places where, in about 3 weeks,
there would be local and regional elections.

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:16.320
But he wasn’t running for office.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.660
He was urging people to vote out the ruling
party, United Russia, led by the president,

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.001
Vladimir Putin.

00:00:33.107 --> 00:00:39.740
He’s made many videos like this before and
they usually rack up millions of views.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:43.611
It’s this ability to reach people via the
internet that has helped make Navalny the

00:00:43.611 --> 00:00:48.040
face of Russia's opposition movement.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.120
Soon after making his case in Siberia, he
got on a plane bound for Moscow.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.050
But the plane was suddenly diverted to Omsk.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.240
Navalny had been poisoned.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.460
And collapsed on the plane.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.610
An investigation later revealed that he had
been poisoned with Novichok, a highly-toxic

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.330
nerve agent, that was developed by the Soviet
Union in the 1970s.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:14.670
It was also used, likely by Russia in 2018
to attack Sergei Skirpal, a former spy.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:19.860
Navalny survived the assassination attempt
and set off a movement unlike any in recent

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:20.860
history.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.210
So how did he do it?

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.578
And why is he such a big threat to Putin?

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:47.450
On December 31, 1999, Putin became the President
of Russia.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.829
The Soviet Union had collapsed just 8 years
earlier and the new Russian Federation was

00:01:51.829 --> 00:01:54.170
slowly transitioning to democracy.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:59.429
Previously, Putin had been a spy in the Soviet
KGB and head of the Russian security service.

00:01:59.429 --> 00:02:02.670
Roles that shaped how he wanted to govern
as President.

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.990
So, he was trying to remake Russia in the image
of the KGB.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:12.000
Like if everything in the world could be as
centralized, insular, and secretive as the

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.260
KGB, it would work well.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.239
So in order to maximize his control, Putin
surrounded himself with the most powerful

00:02:18.239 --> 00:02:20.040
elements in Russia.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.630
Starting with the media.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:26.380
Police were sent into Russia’s independent
media companies, charging their owners, and

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.760
bringing newsrooms under state control.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:33.970
After federal television, it went on to regional
television and then, it went to print newspapers.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:36.640
It was like a flesh eating machine.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.209
Whatever it could see that was functioning
independently, it would gobble up next.

00:02:41.209 --> 00:02:45.010
This hid Putin’s actions from the public
so he was able to go after another powerful

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.959
element - Russia’s elections.

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:50.220
His regime manipulated who could run for office.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.010
And that typically meant Putin’s party and
a few fake candidates, sanctioned by the regime.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:57.530
This was designed to splinter the opposition
vote.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:59.120
And on top of that,

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.709
The vote counting is rigged.

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:06.030
Meaning that it was nearly impossible to run
against Putin or his party.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:10.909
And that United Russia had control of the
central and local governments all across the country.

00:03:10.909 --> 00:03:13.560
But politicians weren’t the only threat
to Putin.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.810
He also went after Russia's oligarchs and
their prominent friends to weed out some powerful

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.920
critics.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.080
In the early 2000s, most of Russia’s wealth
was concentrated in the hands of a few, very

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.439
powerful men.

00:03:25.439 --> 00:03:27.840
Putin protected those that swore loyalty to
him.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.620
And those who didn't were either arrested
on trumped up charges or mysteriously killed.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.459
So, the most common way to get rid of somebody.

00:03:36.459 --> 00:03:38.819
Is to bring them up on embezzlement charges.

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:43.090
Former Russian oil tycoon, Mikhail Khodorkovsky,
has been found guilty of embezzlement.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:46.319
Russian tycoon, Boris Berezovsky, convicted
of embezzlement.

00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:53.609
A former Russian business man, he was accused
of embezzling money, Nikolay Glushkov, the

00:03:53.609 --> 00:03:55.359
cause of death is still unexplained.

00:03:55.359 --> 00:03:58.079
That's how the judiciary is weaponized.

00:03:58.079 --> 00:04:02.609
With the law on his side, within a decade,
Putin insulated himself with the most powerful

00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:05.129
elements on all sides.

00:04:05.129 --> 00:04:06.940
And it was all held together by corruption.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:11.719
Corruption is a structural feature of the
regime.

00:04:11.719 --> 00:04:13.799
It's not an inefficiency of the regime.

00:04:13.799 --> 00:04:15.620
It's not a drag on the regime.

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:19.049
It's the core of the regime.

00:04:19.049 --> 00:04:24.020
According to a report, over $400 billion were
lost to corruption in Russia between 2000

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:25.240
and 2008.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:28.650
But because Putin controlled the media, much
of it was hidden.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:33.680
Until a young lawyer named Alexei Navalny
found a way to change that.

00:04:36.223 --> 00:04:39.960
In 2006, Navalny started a blog where he wrote
about corruption.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:45.009
In 2010, he wrote that at least $4 billion
was stolen out of the state-owned transportation

00:04:45.009 --> 00:04:46.490
company, Transneft.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:47.669
And he had proof.

00:04:47.669 --> 00:04:51.440
He had bought stock in the company and was
able to access internal documents

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.110
that plainly showed how government money was
funneled into offshore accounts owned

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.190
by Transneft officials.

00:04:57.190 --> 00:04:58.870
And this was just the beginning.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:03.479
Navalny soon published investigations on corrupt
oil schemes, land deals, and fraud at state-owned

00:05:03.479 --> 00:05:08.060
banks by Russian oligarchs and politicians.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:12.650
By posting straight to his blog, Navalny was
circumventing state media to reveal corruption

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:17.070
and expose Putin’s regime in a way that
Russians had never seen before.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.410
All the while making a name for himself.

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:21.510
Like he thought that corruption was
something knowable.

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:23.971
That that you could learn about it, you could
systematize it.

00:05:23.971 --> 00:05:26.774
And, again, you could take it seriously. 

00:05:26.774 --> 00:05:28.569
And I said, you know what?

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:34.320
I think we now have an actual politician in
this country.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:40.180
In 2011, huge protests erupted when Putin’s
party won a majority in parliament despite

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.280
reports of voter fraud.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:46.220
It was the largest wave of demonstrations
Putin’s regime had ever faced.

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.020
And Navalny was one of the main organizers.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:53.561
He was building on his investigations by speaking
out publicly against corruption.

00:06:02.569 --> 00:06:06.620
State television ignored the protests even
as the police arrested more than 1,000 people

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.280
and went after the organizers.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:13.925
One of the other ones was jailed, several
were forced into exile, one was murdered,

00:06:13.925 --> 00:06:14.925
Boris Nemstov.

00:06:14.925 --> 00:06:17.009
And Navalny was the last guy standing.

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:21.560
Over time, he developed a talent for organizing
protests and gained a following.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:25.259
Then in 2013, he ran for mayor of Moscow.

00:06:25.259 --> 00:06:27.139
And it stirred some controversy.

00:06:27.139 --> 00:06:30.889
He had participated in Russian nationalist
marches in the past.

00:06:30.889 --> 00:06:33.669
And used ethnic slurs when referring to Russian
minorities.

00:06:33.669 --> 00:06:38.059
But people were still drawn to the main message
of his campaign.

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:52.569
State TV didn’t give his campaign any airtime,
so he relied on rallies, online crowdsourcing,

00:06:52.569 --> 00:06:55.650
and an army of volunteers to spread the word.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:07:00.759
But, just as he was gaining momentum, police
arrested him on trumped-up charges of embezzlement

00:07:00.759 --> 00:07:03.110
and sentenced him to 5 years in jail.

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:06.470
His supporters flooded the streets in protest.

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:10.889
Navalny was eventually released on bail and
didn't win the election.

00:07:10.889 --> 00:07:13.750
But, he went on to finish second place.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:16.430
He continued to expose corruption through
Youtube videos.

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:21.633
Like this one, in 2017, on the extreme wealth
of Putin's ally, Dmitri Medvedev.

00:07:31.042 --> 00:07:34.419
It reached millions and sparked another round
of protests.

00:07:34.419 --> 00:07:36.720
At the same time, Navalny was running for
office again.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.373
This time, against Putin for President.

00:07:44.346 --> 00:07:49.500
Predictably, the regime struck back and disqualified
him based on the previous embezzlement charges.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.810
Further revealing just how rigged Russia’s
laws and elections were.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:58.130
But in 2020, Navalny found a way to take on
the whole electoral system.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:07:59.699
He called it Smart-Voting.

00:07:59.699 --> 00:08:04.159
Instead of letting the opposition vote splinter
among several dummy candidates, Navalny identified

00:08:04.159 --> 00:08:08.780
one candidate and urged people to vote for
the same one, even if they were backed by

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:09.870
Putin's regime.

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:15.272
It takes an incredible amount of public trust
and charisma to get people to

00:08:15.272 --> 00:08:17.199
unify behind a meaningless candidate.

00:08:17.199 --> 00:08:23.978
But, its true, if you get enough people to do it, it
actually can add up to meaningful resistance.

00:08:23.978 --> 00:08:26.016
And it terrifies the Kremlin.

00:08:26.016 --> 00:08:30.409
During that time, Navalny was the second most
popular politician in all of Russia according

00:08:30.409 --> 00:08:31.409
to some polls.

00:08:31.409 --> 00:08:36.260
While Putin’s favorability ratings were
slipping because of a struggling economy.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:37.550
That made Navalny a threat.

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:40.890
And it explains why he was poisoned, possibly
more than once.

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:46.250
Once he was very weirdly ill and once his
wife, Yulia, was very weirdly ill.

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:52.940
They were looking for an opportunity, a place
and a time and it finally seemed like it was

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:53.940
almost perfect.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:58.011
But I think they did expect that they would be
rid of him by now and they're not.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:04.610
After the Siberia attack, Navalny recovered
in a German hospital where he miraculously

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:05.610
survived.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:08.950
A few months later, he went back to his investigations.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:13.329
In December 2020, he tricked the secret agent
who poisoned him into revealing how he did

00:09:13.329 --> 00:09:14.293
it.

00:09:25.232 --> 00:09:29.339
Then, in January, he returned to Russia knowing
he would be arrested.

00:09:29.339 --> 00:09:33.610
Police met him at the airport, and charged
him with violating the parole from his 2014

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:34.770
embezzlement case.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:39.839
While detained, his team released another
video, this one attacking Putin directly.

00:09:48.483 --> 00:09:51.730
It’s been viewed over 100 million times.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:55.250
His supporters flooded the streets in over
100 cities across Russia.

00:09:55.250 --> 00:10:00.350
A few days later, Navalny was sentenced to
2 years and 8 months in prison, sparking even

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:02.529
more protests.

00:10:02.529 --> 00:10:07.990
Police have arrested more than 5,000 people
while state media has downplayed it.

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:12.700
Even though Navalny ended up in prison again,
his movement continued to play out on the

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:13.700
streets.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:17.420
By exposing Putin's regime for a decade, Navalny
might have found a way to build a movement

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:20.138
that could outlast his freedom.

